---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import AashaCare from './AashaCare.astro';
import AashaQuestions from './AashaQuestions.astro';
import aasha from '../data/aasha.json';
import { visitQuestions } from '../data/aasha-visit-questions';
interface Props { slug: string }
const service = aasha.services.find(item => item.slug === Astro.props.slug);
if (!service) throw new Error(`Unknown Aasha service: ${Astro.props.slug}`);
const canonical = `${aasha.url}/${service.slug}`;
const escape = (value: string) => value.replaceAll('&', '&amp;').replaceAll('"', '&quot;').replaceAll('<', '&lt;');
const headExtra = `<title>${escape(service.title)}</title><meta name="description" content="${escape(service.metaDescription ?? service.answer)}"><link rel="canonical" href="${canonical}"><link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/css/min/seo-pages.min.css">`;
const schema = JSON.stringify({ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@graph': [
  { '@type': 'Service', '@id': `${canonical}#service`, name: service.name, serviceType: service.name, description: service.answer, url: canonical, mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': `${canonical}#webpage` }, provider: { '@id': aasha.id }, areaServed: { '@type': 'City', name: 'Greater Noida' } },
  { '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [{ '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://mindgracencr.in/' }, { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: aasha.shortName, item: aasha.url }, { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: service.name, item: canonical }] }
] }).replaceAll('<', '\\u003c');
---
<Layout headExtra={headExtra}>
  <main id="main-content" class="seo-page">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true"> / </span><a href="/aasha">Aasha</a><span aria-hidden="true"> / </span><span aria-current="page">{service.name}</span></nav>
    <section class="seo-hero">
      <p class="seo-kicker">Aasha · Gamma 2, Greater Noida</p>
      <h1>{service.name} in Greater Noida</h1>
      <p class="seo-answer">{service.answer}</p>
      {service.slug === 'developmental-assessment' && <p><a href="/aasha/developmental-check-in">Prepare for your visit with a developmental check-in</a> — organise your observations about one child, then print or choose to share a summary.</p>}
      <div class="seo-actions"><a class="btn" href={`tel:${aasha.telephone}`}>Call to book an assessment</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="/aasha/book">Request an Aasha appointment</a></div>
    </section>
    <section class="seo-section"><h2>{service.heading}</h2><ul>{service.areas.map(area => <li>{area}</li>)}</ul><p>Each child’s plan is individualised. The team discusses appropriate goals with the family and reviews progress over time, adjusting the plan when a goal stops being useful rather than pushing a child to meet it at any cost.</p></section>
    <section class="seo-section"><h2>What to expect at Aasha</h2><p>{service.process}</p><p>Read about <a href="/aasha#leadership">our clinical leadership</a> and <a href="/aasha#journey">the consultation-to-therapy care pathway</a>.</p></section>
    <section class="seo-section"><h2>Preparing for your first appointment</h2><p>{service.prepare}</p><p>Confirm the assessment scope, session arrangements and current fees with the coordination team before booking. Families can ask questions about the proposed plan and how parents can participate.</p><a href={service.related.href}>{service.related.label}</a></section>
    {service.slug === 'speech-language-therapy' && <section class="seo-section">
      <h2>When should I ask for a speech and language assessment?</h2>
      <p>Start with a moment that is hard for your child. Perhaps your child knows what they want at breakfast but cannot ask in a way other people understand. A teacher might say your child misses instructions during group activities, even though similar directions are easy at home. An older child might have plenty to say but avoid speaking with friends who often ask them to repeat themselves. Write down what happened, who was there, how your child communicated and what helped. These details give the clinician something specific to investigate.</p>
      <h3>Speech, language and communication</h3>
      <p>Speech concerns how sounds and words are produced. Language includes understanding and expressing meaning. Communication also includes gesture, pointing, pictures and devices. A child can speak clearly and still struggle to follow a story or explain an idea. Another child may understand a great deal and find it hard to produce words unfamiliar listeners recognize. The next step depends on the pattern and its effect on everyday life. See the <a href="https://www.asha.org/practice-portal/Clinical-Topics/Spoken-Language-Disorders/">ASHA guidance on spoken language disorders</a>.</p>
      <h2>What might the therapist look at during a first visit?</h2>
      <p>The clinician may ask about development, hearing, health, school and the languages your child hears and uses. Think about who understands your child most easily, when conversation flows naturally and what changes when a room is noisy or a conversation moves quickly. Share things that may never happen during a short visit, such as how your child requests a favourite song or tells you about something that happened at school.</p>
      <p>The therapist may observe play, conversation or structured tasks depending on your child's age and comfort. A younger child might show how they take turns, request an object or follow a familiar routine. An older child might describe a picture or explain the rules of a game. Ask what the clinician can learn from the visit, what questions remain and whether a hearing assessment or another evaluation would help. If your child has lost previously established words or gestures, tell the team promptly.</p>
      <h3>What if my child uses Hindi and English?</h3>
      <p>Tell the therapist which languages your child hears, who uses each language and what your child understands or says in each setting. Include languages used with grandparents, caregivers and teachers. A child may know foods in one language and classroom vocabulary in another. Bring examples from home and school if you have them. Ask how the clinician will account for language exposure and what information they need if a language used at home differs from the language used in the clinic.</p>
      <h2>What could a useful speech therapy goal look like?</h2>
      <p>Choose a situation that matters to your child and agree on what would change. A broad aim like improving communication is easier to work on when everyone can describe the routine, the support offered and the result they hope to see.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Asking for help in play.</strong> When a container is hard to open, your child could show a help card, gesture, say a word or use their device. Notice whether the child can make the request with different familiar adults.</li>
        <li><strong>Repairing a misunderstood message.</strong> A child whose speech is hard to understand could practise a useful word and learn to repair the message by pointing, showing the object or trying a different word.</li>
        <li><strong>Following a classroom routine.</strong> The family and teacher can compare how the child responds to a spoken instruction alone and with a picture cue. Share which support makes the routine easier.</li>
        <li><strong>Taking part in a conversation.</strong> Pick a topic the child enjoys and a partner they feel comfortable with. Practise adding one detail or asking a question that the child actually wants answered.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Ask how the therapist will record progress and when you will review the goals together. Progress might mean more independent requests, fewer misunderstandings or using a skill with a new person. Bring examples from a regular week so the clinician can see whether a skill practised in the clinic is useful in everyday life.</p>
      <h2>How can speech and language therapy support autistic children?</h2>
      <p>Start with the ways your child already communicates and identify where additional options could help. Priorities could include refusing an uncomfortable activity, asking for a break, sharing an interest or recovering when someone misunderstands. Some autistic children repeat words or phrases they have heard. Observe when the repetition occurs, what happened before it and how your child responds to the listener. This gives the therapist context for understanding what the phrase might communicate. The <a href="https://www.asha.org/Practice-Portal/Clinical-Topics/Autism/">ASHA autism practice guide</a> discusses echolalia and different forms of communication.</p>
      <p>Augmentative and alternative communication, or AAC, includes options such as gestures, picture boards and speech generating devices. A child who speaks in some situations can still use another method when speech is harder. Ask how the proposed option will be available during meals, play and school, and how adults can model it during ordinary conversation. See the <a href="https://www.asha.org/practice-portal/professional-issues/augmentative-and-alternative-communication">ASHA AAC guide</a> for examples.</p>
      <p>Read the <a href="/aasha/echolalia">echolalia guide</a> for examples of immediate and delayed repetition and ways to respond in everyday routines.</p>
<h2>What can families do between visits?</h2>
      <p>Pick one manageable routine with the therapist. At snack time, offer a choice, pause to give your child time and acknowledge how they communicate their choice. During play, follow what interests them and model a short phrase or gesture that fits what they are doing. Ask which cues are helpful and when you should offer less help. A short note about what worked and whether your child seemed comfortable is enough to start the next conversation with the therapist.</p>
      <h2>How can I plan the cost of speech therapy in Greater Noida?</h2>
      <p>Ask for the current assessment fee, what feedback it includes and the price per therapy session. Ask how often the clinician proposes meeting, what practice is manageable at home and when progress and session frequency will be reviewed. Compare the expected cost through that first review as well as the price of a single session. If travel, school hours or fees limit what your family can manage, raise that when discussing a schedule.</p>
      <p>Aasha is in Gamma 2, Greater Noida. Use <a href="/aasha/book">Aasha booking</a> to ask about availability, fees and what to bring. If your concerns span communication, movement and learning, read about <a href="/aasha/developmental-assessment">developmental assessment</a> before choosing a service.</p>
    </section>}
    {service.slug === 'occupational-therapy' && <section class="seo-section">
      <h2>When should I ask about occupational therapy for my child?</h2>
      <p>Consider an assessment when a routine your child needs or wants to take part in regularly becomes difficult. Examples include getting dressed, opening a school lunchbox, using playground equipment, joining a craft activity or staying comfortable during a school assembly. Describe what the child is trying to do, which part is difficult, and whether changing the materials, timing or surroundings helps. A useful assessment starts with the routine and the child's experience of it.</p>
      <h2>What happens during a pediatric OT assessment?</h2>
      <p>The therapist may ask you to describe a typical day and identify one or two activities that matter most to your family. Bring school observations, previous reports or a sample of schoolwork if they help explain the concern. For a dressing difficulty, describe the clothing involved, whether the child can start the task, which steps need help, and what happens when there is more time. For classroom writing, describe the amount of work expected, how long your child can write comfortably and whether they can explain the same ideas aloud.</p>
      <p>The therapist can then look at the task itself. They may observe how the child handles objects, coordinates movements, responds to textures and sounds, or uses help from another person. A child may have difficulty with buttons because the fabric is stiff, the movement is unfamiliar, the shirt feels uncomfortable or the morning is rushed. Each explanation suggests a different next step. Ask the therapist what they observed and what they still need to learn before proposing a plan.</p>
      <h2>How can occupational therapy help autistic children?</h2>
      <p>Occupational therapy can support autistic children with everyday activities such as play, dressing and school participation. An autistic child who finds assemblies overwhelming might benefit from a predictable seat, permission to step outside, or a way to signal that they need a break. A child who enjoys movement might find some activities easier when the routine allows time to move. The child's preferences and response help the family and therapist decide which support is useful.</p>
      <p>Talk with the therapist about what your child wants to do more comfortably and what changes the adults around them can make. For example, a family might prioritize choosing clothes the child can tolerate and practising one fastening when the child is comfortable. A teacher could offer another way to record ideas while the therapist looks at writing comfort and access. <a href="https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg170/chapter/recommendations">NICE guidance on autism support</a> discusses adapting environments and supporting daily living skills.</p>
      <h2>What does progress look like in occupational therapy?</h2>
      <p>Agree on a goal that describes the activity, the setting and the level of help. If opening a lunchbox matters, the first goal might be for the child to open a container they can grip during a relaxed snack at home. The family and teacher can then check whether the child can use it at school. If the school setting is harder, ask what changes there. The child may need a different container, a place to set it down or more time before lunch ends.</p>
      <p>For handwriting, progress could mean writing a short response with less fatigue or being able to finish a classroom task using a suitable alternative. For dressing, it might mean the child can put on a preferred shirt with a verbal cue and ask for help with a fastening. Record changes that matter to the child, including comfort, willingness to try and how much help they want. Review whether the skill carries over to the real routine before increasing the difficulty.</p>
      <h2>Should we start with OT or speech and language therapy?</h2>
      <p>Look at what is getting in the way of participation. A child who can ask for lunch but struggles to open the box might start with OT. A child who opens it independently but cannot ask for help or explain that they need something different might benefit from a speech and language assessment. Some children have both concerns. Ask how the clinicians would coordinate around one shared goal and what each would contribute. Read more about <a href="/aasha/speech-language-therapy">Aasha speech and language therapy</a>.</p>
      <h2>How can we make an OT plan manageable?</h2>
      <p>Ask for the assessment fee, session cost, proposed frequency and first review point before agreeing to a schedule. Include travel, school time and any equipment costs in the discussion. Ask which activities you can practise with materials already at home, what signs tell you to pause, and what changes would lead the therapist to adjust the plan. Try one activity that fits your normal week and share what you observe at the next visit.</p>
      <p>For occupational therapy in Gamma 2, Greater Noida, use <a href="/aasha/book">Aasha booking</a> to confirm availability, current fees and what to bring. If your concerns span several developmental areas, read about <a href="/aasha/developmental-assessment">developmental assessment</a>.</p>
    </section>}
    {visitQuestions[service.slug]?.map(section => <section class="seo-section"><h2>{section.heading}</h2>{section.paragraphs.map(paragraph => <p>{paragraph}</p>)}</section>)}
    <section class="seo-section"><h2>Plan your next step</h2><ul><li><a href="/aasha/autism-early-intervention">Choosing early intervention support for an autistic child</a></li><li><a href="/aasha/therapy-costs">Questions about assessment and therapy costs</a></li></ul></section>
    <AashaQuestions items={[service.faq, aasha.faqs[0], aasha.faqs[1]]} />
    <section class="seo-section"><h2>Related Aasha services</h2><div class="seo-grid">{aasha.services.filter(item => item.slug !== service.slug).map(item => <article class="seo-card"><h3><a href={`/aasha/${item.slug}`}>{item.name}</a></h3><p>{item.answer}</p></article>)}</div></section>
    <AashaCare />
  </main>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={schema}></script>
</Layout>
